== B. Derivatives

=== C7 \[Core\] Zero-Volatility Call \/ 零波动率看涨期权
*中文：* 无分红 Black–Scholes 市场中，$S_0=K=100$、连续复利率 $r=0.05$（即 5\%）、期限 1 年且波动率为 0。欧式看涨价值是多少？卖方如何对冲？

*English:* In a non-dividend Black–Scholes market, $S_0=K=100$, the continuously compounded rate is $r=0.05$ (5\%), maturity is one year, and volatility is zero. What is the European call worth, and how should a short call be hedged?

~~~
{}{raw}
<details class="answer"><summary>Solution</summary><div class="answer-content">
<p>The stock grows deterministically at the risk-free rate under the model, so the call finishes in the money. Its value is</p><p class="equation-line">$$C_0=S_0-Ke^{-rT}=100(1-e^{-0.05})\approx4.8771.$$</p><p>Its delta is one. A short call is hedged by holding one share and borrowing $Ke^{-rT}$; the portfolio exactly delivers the terminal payoff.</p>
</div></details>
~~~

=== C8 \[Core\] Equal Call and Put Values \/ 同价看涨与看跌
*中文：* 同一无分红股票上的欧式看涨和看跌具有相同 $K,T$，且 $S_0=K$、$r=0$。看涨上行无限而看跌上行有限，为什么二者价格仍相等？

*English:* A European call and put share $K,T$ on a non-dividend stock, with $S_0=K$ and $r=0$. The call has unlimited upside while the put does not. Why must their prices nevertheless be equal?

~~~
{}{raw}
<details class="answer"><summary>Solution</summary><div class="answer-content">
<p>Put–call parity gives</p><p class="equation-line">$$C-P=S_0-Ke^{-rT}=0,$$</p><p>so $C=P$. The payoff ranges alone do not determine value: the risk-neutral probabilities and payoff magnitudes differ across states. Pointwise, call minus put equals $S_T-K$, and the prepaid forward value of that difference is zero here.</p>
</div></details>
~~~

=== C9 \[Medium\] Rebalancing a Long-Call Hedge \/ 多头看涨的对冲调整
*中文：* 你持有一份看涨期权，并通过卖空 $\\Delta$ 股股票做 Delta 中性对冲。股价突然大跌后，一般应买入还是继续卖空股票？为什么？

*English:* You own a call and delta-hedge it by shorting $\\Delta$ shares. After a sharp stock-price drop, should you generally buy or sell stock to rebalance, and why?

~~~
{}{raw}
<details class="answer"><summary>Solution</summary><div class="answer-content">
<p>A standard call has positive gamma, so its delta falls when the stock falls. The required short-stock hedge becomes smaller in magnitude; therefore <strong>buy back shares</strong>. A long-gamma rehedging rule buys after declines and sells after rises. Jumps, discrete trading, costs, and model error prevent perfect replication.</p>
</div></details>
~~~

=== C10 \[Medium\] Cash-or-Nothing Digital Call \/ 现金或无数字看涨
*中文：* 到期若 $S_T>K$ 就支付固定金额 $H$，否则支付 0。Black–Scholes 模型下该欧式数字期权的价格是多少？它与普通看涨对执行价的导数有什么关系？

*English:* A European digital call pays $H$ if $S_T>K$ and zero otherwise. What is its Black–Scholes price, and how is it related to the strike derivative of a vanilla call?

~~~
{}{raw}
<details class="answer"><summary>Solution</summary><div class="answer-content">
<p>Under the risk-neutral measure, with continuous dividend yield $q$,</p><p class="equation-line">$$D=He^{-r\tau}N(d_2),\qquad d_2=\frac{\log(S/K)+(r-q-\tfrac12\sigma^2)\tau}{\sigma\sqrt{\tau}}.$$</p><p>For a vanilla call $C(K)$ with the same maturity,</p><p class="equation-line">$$-\frac{\partial C}{\partial K}=e^{-r\tau}N(d_2),$$</p><p>so $D=-H\,\partial C/\partial K$ where differentiability holds.</p>
</div></details>
~~~

=== C11 \[Core\] Early Exercise of a Call \/ 看涨期权提前行权
*中文：* 对无分红股票，在非负利率与无摩擦市场中，为什么美式看涨不应提前行权？哪些现实因素可能改变结论？

*English:* Why should an American call on a non-dividend stock not be exercised early under nonnegative rates and frictionless trading? What real-world features can alter the conclusion?

~~~
{}{raw}
<details class="answer"><summary>Solution</summary><div class="answer-content">
<p>Early exercise discards time value and pays $K$ sooner. Keeping or selling the option while investing the present value of $K$ weakly dominates. Hence $C_A=C_E$ under the assumptions. Dividends, negative rates, funding or short-sale constraints, transaction costs, and special contract terms can change the comparison.</p>
</div></details>
~~~

=== C12 \[Medium\] Smile and Gamma–Theta \/ 微笑与 Gamma–Theta
*中文：* （a）为何不同执行价的隐含波动率形成微笑或偏斜？（b）为什么香草期权的 Gamma 与 Theta 经常符号相反？

*English:* (a) Why does implied volatility vary by strike as a smile or skew? (b) Why do gamma and theta often have opposite signs for vanilla options?

~~~
{}{raw}
<details class="answer"><summary>Solution</summary><div class="answer-content">
<p>(a) A constant-lognormal model cannot match market-implied skewness, heavy tails, jumps, stochastic volatility, leverage effects, and option supply-demand, so prices invert to strike-dependent implied volatilities. (b) The Black–Scholes PDE</p><p class="equation-line">$$\Theta+\frac12\sigma^2S^2\Gamma+rS\Delta-rV=0$$</p><p>shows that, after abstracting from delta and carry, long convexity is financed by time decay. The sign relation is a useful vanilla approximation, not a universal rule for every portfolio or exotic payoff.</p>
</div></details>
~~~

